CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure presents six diagrams of the three cubic unit cell types — simple cubic, body-centered cubic (BCC), and face-centered cubic (FCC) — arranged in two rows of three: a top row titled "Lattice point locations" showing schematic dot-and-line cube diagrams, and a bottom row titled "Cubic unit cells" showing space-filling sphere models, with each column sharing a label (Simple cubic, Body-centered cubic, Face-centered cubic) at the bottom. In the top-row schematic diagrams, the simple cubic cell has black dots only at the eight corners; the BCC cell adds one red dot at the body center; and the FCC cell adds red dots at the center of each face while retaining black dots at all corners. In the bottom-row space-filling models, the simple cubic unit cell shows gray corner spheres touching along cube edges with no central atom; the BCC model highlights the single body-center atom in orange, showing it in contact with all eight gray corner atoms; and the FCC model shows multiple orange face-centered atoms contacting both the gray corner atoms and neighboring face atoms along face diagonals. By pairing each abstract lattice-point diagram directly above its corresponding space-filling model, the figure makes explicit how lattice geometry translates into actual atomic packing and allows the number of atoms per unit cell to be counted for each type. This figure introduces the three cubic unit cells as the fundamental repeating units of metallic crystal lattices and establishes the atom-counting framework needed to calculate density and relate crystal structure to bulk properties.

PROJECTION TYPE: oblique
Call setCameraView({ projection: 'oblique', obliqueAngleDeg: 45, depthScale: 0.5, heightFraction }) after all geometry is added — a standard cabinet-oblique pose; the plan does not measure per-figure oblique angles. The scaffold implements oblique as a shear of the eye-space projection matrix, so the front face stays undistorted, depth edges leave at obliqueAngleDeg, and labels track the shear automatically. Build the TRUE 3D object — the shear is the camera's job. Do NOT shear geometry, do NOT wrap the figure in a sheared parent Object3D, and do NOT disable automatic matrix updates. The scaffold also provides the reveal interaction: dragging eases the shear out to expose the real 3D shape and a toggle button returns to the printed view — do not implement either yourself.
AXIS ASSIGNMENT: the pose is pinned, so in BoxGeometry(w, h, d) the w axis is screen-right, h is screen-up, and d is the receding diagonal. Before writing each BoxGeometry, look at the source image and decide which of the object's dimensions actually recedes there — do not assume it is the one the domain calls "depth". In feature-map / layer-stack diagrams the channel count is usually the w (horizontal) extent along the flow direction and a spatial dimension is d. Sanity-check the silhouette against the source: a block that should read as a long bar pointing at its neighbours must be long in w, not in d, or it will render as a sliver shooting into the page. If GEOMETRY NOTES assigns the axes in a way that contradicts the source image's silhouette, follow the image.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Top panel title text 'Lattice point locations'
  - Top panel: skeletal cube with 12 black edge lines and translucent pale-green faces (top face lightest, front face mid-green, right face darkest)
  - Top panel: 8 black spheres/dots at the cube corners (lattice points)
  - Top panel: 1 red dot at the body center (BCC mode only)
  - Top panel: 6 red dots at the centers of the six cube faces (FCC mode only)
  - Bottom panel title text 'Cubic unit cells'
  - Bottom panel: same translucent pale-green cube with black wireframe edges, drawn over/through the spheres
  - Bottom panel: 8 large gray shaded spheres centered on the cube corners (only the in-cube octants visually dominate, spheres bulge outside the cell)
  - Bottom panel: 1 orange sphere at the body center touching all 8 gray corner spheres (BCC mode)
  - Bottom panel: 6 orange spheres at the six face centers touching corner spheres along face diagonals (FCC mode)
  - Column caption text below bottom panel: 'Simple cubic' / 'Body-centered cubic' / 'Face-centered cubic'
  - Atom-count readout panel: corner 8 × 1/8, face 6 × 1/2, body 1 × 1, total atoms per unit cell
  - Highlight ring/wedge overlay showing the fraction of a clicked sphere that lies inside the cell

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Two stacked sub-scenes sharing one cube edge length a = 2.0 world units. Top 'Lattice point locations' cube centered at (0, 2.4, 0); bottom 'Cubic unit cells' cube centered at (0, -2.0, 0). Cube axes aligned to +X (screen-right), +Y (screen-up), +Z (receding diagonal up-right, cabinet-oblique at roughly 30-40° with ~0.6 depth foreshortening in the source). Faces are translucent green (opacity ~0.55): top face #e4f1d2, front face #cde5ab, right face #a9cd7f; all 12 edges solid black, ~2 px. Top-panel corner markers: black spheres radius 0.09 at the 8 corners (±1, ±1, ±1). Interior lattice markers: red #e8231a spheres radius 0.07 — one at (0,0,0) for BCC; six at (±1,0,0),(0,±1,0),(0,0,±1) for FCC. Bottom-panel sphere radii at full size: simple cubic corner r = a/2 = 1.00 (touch along edge); BCC r = √3·a/4 = 0.866 for BOTH the gray corners and the orange body-center atom; FCC r = √2·a/4 = 0.707 for gray corners and orange face atoms. Gray spheres #9b9b9e with soft specular shading and darker limb; orange spheres #d2491a, same shading style; orange spheres are drawn in front of / intersecting the gray ones, and spheres visibly bulge outside the cube faces. The green cube faces and black wireframe are drawn over the spheres (faces semi-transparent so spheres show through). Atom counts: SC = 8×1/8 = 1; BCC = 8×1/8 + 1 = 2; FCC = 8×1/8 + 6×1/2 = 4. Caption text baseline ~1.6 units below the bottom cube; panel titles ~1.5 units above each cube, bold dark-gray/black sans-serif.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "cellType",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "How adding lattice points at the body center or at every face center changes both the abstract lattice diagram and the real atomic packing, while the cube itself stays the same",
    "label": "Cubic unit cell",
    "options": [
      "Simple cubic",
      "Body-centered cubic",
      "Face-centered cubic"
    ],
    "default": "Simple cubic"
  },
  {
    "id": "clickAtom",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "That an atom is shared between neighboring cells — a corner atom contributes 1/8, a face atom 1/2, a body-center atom 1 — so the totals are 1, 2 and 4 atoms per cell"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The true three-dimensional positions of the lattice points and the contact directions between spheres (edge contact for SC, body-diagonal for BCC, face-diagonal for FCC)"
  },
  {
    "id": "atomRadius",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "The link between the ball-and-stick lattice picture and the space-filling picture: growing the spheres from points until they touch reveals which direction the atoms make contact in each structure",
    "label": "Sphere size (fraction of touching radius)",
    "range": [
      0.15,
      1,
      0.05
    ],
    "default": 1
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Simple cubic",
    "narration": "Lattice points sit only at the eight corners. In the space-filling model the gray spheres touch along the cube edges, so the edge length equals two atomic radii.",
    "state": {
      "cellType": "Simple cubic",
      "atomRadius": 1
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Shrink the spheres",
    "narration": "Pulling the spheres back to small balls shows that the space-filling model and the dot diagram above mark exactly the same eight positions.",
    "state": {
      "cellType": "Simple cubic",
      "atomRadius": 0.3
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Body-centered cubic",
    "narration": "One extra lattice point (red dot) appears at the center of the cube. The orange body-center atom touches all eight corner atoms along the body diagonal, giving 8 × 1/8 + 1 = 2 atoms per cell.",
    "state": {
      "cellType": "Body-centered cubic",
      "atomRadius": 1
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Face-centered cubic",
    "narration": "Red lattice points now sit at the center of every face. Each orange face atom is shared by two cells, so the count is 8 × 1/8 + 6 × 1/2 = 4 atoms per cell, with contact along the face diagonals.",
    "state": {
      "cellType": "Face-centered cubic",
      "atomRadius": 1
    }
  }
]

AXIS ANGLES: null — null means the source is a flat 2D figure; pass axisScreenAngles: null to setCameraView for a front-on view.

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.72
}

NOTES: Render spheres as MeshStandardMaterial with one key directional light upper-left plus ambient to reproduce the shaded-ball look; use depthWrite:false + renderOrder on the translucent green faces so spheres behind them remain visible. Draw cube edges with LineSegments (or Line2 for constant width) on top of geometry. For clickAtom use raycasting on the sphere meshes: on hit, emissive-highlight the sphere, dim the others, and draw the in-cell fraction by clipping a copy of the sphere to the cube with three clipping planes (THREE.Plane) so the learner literally sees the 1/8 octant, 1/2 hemisphere, or whole sphere; show a floating label '1/8 of this atom is inside' plus a running tally. The atomRadius slider simply scales sphere geometry between 0.15× and 1× of the structure-specific touching radius. Toggle transitions should lerp sphere radii and fade the red/orange extra atoms in rather than snapping. Titles and captions as sprite/HTML overlay labels that stay screen-facing during orbit.

Follow the interaction plan above.

